00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:02.690 align:start position:0%
 
the 1990s brought a tidal wave of  sitcoms crashing onto the shores of TV  some were amazing  others were terrible but all of them  eventually came to an end  keep watching to discover the most  interesting stories of 90s sitcom  cancellation news radio was one of the  most acclaimed sitcoms of the decade but  all of its positive critical notice came  in spite of or perhaps because of  battles with its network creator Paul  Simms fought with NBC over everything  from scheduling to plot lines by his  telling NBC wanted more established  stars they Foley Andy Dick and Joe Rogan  were all well known in the world of  comedy but they weren't exactly  household names throughout America in  1997 Simms who was almost certain that  his show was getting canceled gave a  profanity-laced  interview to Rolling Stone he admonished  NBC for not giving news radio a slot on  Thursday night's must-see TV lineup and  he called out executives by name  afterwards the show was briefly  cancelled before being brought back  which led to everyone awkwardly walking  back what they said and playing nice the  dynamic of the show changed  significantly in season five following  the death of Phil Hartman he was  something of a big brother to the rest  of the cast and addressing his death  resulted in an uncomfortable tonal shift  Hartman's old SNL castmate Jon Lovitz  was added to the news radio cast but he  never quite clicked it also didn't help  that Preston Beckman NBC's head of  scheduling at the time despised the show  during news radios run it changed time  slots 11 times amidst all this turmoil  it was canceled after the fifth season  sports night Aaron Sorkin's dramedy  about the behind the scenes of a  SportsCenter type show was never a rated  success but it almost didn't matter  it was critically acclaimed it won  awards and the viewers who did watch it  were a passionate Bunch when fans got a  sense that sports night wasn't long for  this world they set up websites  petitioning to save it when ABC axed the  show several other networks including  USA HBO and Showtime expressed interest  in picking it up negotiations with  Showtime went furthest with Sorkin  claiming that they offered him about 37  million dollars for two more seasons  that much money on a network where  ratings weren't critically important was  an almost perfect scenario but alas  Sorkin had no choice but to turn it down  that was because during sports nights  second season he  created a little show called the west  wing for NBC he did almost all of the  writing for both shows splitting his  days between the two of them also around  this time his wife was pregnant and he  realized he couldn't give his all to  sports night anymore  Showtime didn't want to enter into a  heavy commitment without Sorkin going  all-in  so negotiations ended home-improvement  was without a doubt one of the most  popular sitcoms of the 90s it spent its  entire eighth season run rated in the  top ten in the series finale aired on a  third of all TVs in America but there  was never a 9th season because stars Tim  Allen and patricia richardson both  wanted to move on richardson was offered  30 million dollars for another year but  she turned it down because she decided  to focus on her family she was a single  mother to three children and  home-improvement was keeping her away  from them too much Allen meanwhile was  at the most bankable point in his career  during his run on home improvement he  starred in major box-office hits like  the Santa Claus Toy Story and jungle 2  jungle Hollywood was calling and besides  no matter how much he was offered he  didn't want to do the show without  Richardson Wow I just shows how smart  you are long before she played a  neurobiologist on The Big Bang Theory  Mayim Bialik was an average teenage girl  on the family sitcom blossom the show  had a dedicated audience before it ended  after five seasons it was canceled for  three main reasons the stars were  getting older the audience was aging out  and it had tough competition in the form  of Melrose Place the thing about shows  focused on teenagers is that they always  grow up eventually the final episode of  blossom sees the title character getting  ready for college and a life utterly  unlike the one fans fell in love with  the primary audience for these shows  tends to be tween girls and their tastes  change in mature as they grow up so  shows like blossom tend to have a shelf  life especially when more mature  programs like Melrose Place around  blossom for its part did tackle some  touchy subjects that became part of the  show's DNA this kept it relevant but it  couldn't save it entirely kids learn  some important lessons from blossom  about topics like divorce and addiction  but then they grew past it dinosaurs was  a strange show there's no other way to  put it in many ways it was a normal  sitcom about a suburban family dealing  with everyday issues except for the fact  that every cast member was a dinosaur  puppet the show nevertheless somehow had  some of the sharpest writing on TV of  its era and it was without question one  of the most visually interesting shows  of the decade alas the cost of dinosaurs  sophisticated puppetry ultimately made  it unsustainable  especially when ratings started to dip  rumours spread that it was the most  expensive half-hour on television  eventually it just wasn't worth what it  cost to make and so ABC cancelled it but  the writers weren't interested in going  gently into the good night as they  decided to make one of the most bonkers  series finales in TV history entitled  changing nature the episode sees the  dinosaurs try to handle an invasive  plant species by accidentally setting  off an ice age that leads to the  extinction of the entire species the  script prompted a call from ABC's  president asking the writers where they  got the hutzpah to kill off the entire  cast the writers responded that they  were inspired by the planets actual  history in the words of series creator  Michael Jacobs when you do a show about  dinosaurs you always have that  extinction card in your pocket and we  wanted to use it Ellen made television  history in 1997 with the puppy episode  in which the main character played by  Ellen DeGeneres comes out of the closet  Susan I'm gay  while this has endured is a landmark  moment in TV history it also proves to  be the beginning of the end for the show  as a combination of backlash and weird  creative decisions cost ratings too  plummets during Ellen's fourth season  DeGeneres and ABC were fighting about  the show's content network executives  were reluctant to let Ellen come out and  they started putting content warnings  before episodes DeGeneres objected to  this and threatened to walk before  making a fifth season Media discussion  about the show after the puppy episode  aired was growing more and more complex  Chaz Bono at the time the media director  of GLAAD worried that the show was  leaning in a little too much as he put  it it's one thing to have a gay lead  character but it's another when every  episode deals with pretty specific gay  issues ultimately Ellen was cancelled  after five seasons though it's legacy  lives on to this day grace under fire  was an early project for Chuck Lorre who  would go on to create such series as Two  and a Half Men and the Big Bang Theory  like many of his future shows it was a  raiding success the title character a  divorced mother and recovering alcoholic  who's played by Brett Butler the show  soared on the wings of her talent and  eventually struggled as her real-life  problems intruded on to the set the  departure of two cast members and five  executive producers were all attributed  to Butler's erratic behavior and drug  addiction during a crossover event  production teams from other shows were  especially horrified the crew for The  Drew Carey Show even got t-shirts that  said I survived Brett grace under fire  productions was halted on at least two  occasions for Butler to attend rehab  both times resulted in long delays both  added to her bad reputation and both  contributed to a ratings decline  eventually ABC got fed up and pulled the  plug Butler is now clean and sober and  has expressed remorse for how she  behaved at the time salute your shorts  was one of Nickelodeon's first big  live-action success stories following a  group of kids at a summer camp it paved  the way for the success of future  sitcoms aimed at kids in the following  decades two seasons were produced  successfully on a shoestring budget but  then production changes forced the  cancellation in June 1990 Nickelodeon  studios opened in Orlando Florida  going forward the network intended to  have all of its programming  there salute your shorts have been  recorded in Los Angeles up to that point  and it struggled to adapt to the change  in location the cast was primarily  children and teenagers who all decided  that it wasn't worth it to move across  the country Hey Dude one of Nick's other  live action comedies face the same  predicaments ultimately with nobody  moving both shows were canceled The  Nanny was so popular in the 90s that  anyone who watched back then can still  hear Fran Drescher's laugh right now  from sense-memory  despite lasting six seasons the show's  cancellation came as something of a  shock  years later Drescher attributed it to  her character Fran fine finally acting  on a romantic tension with Max Sheffield  will they won't they storylines permeate  many long-running sitcoms and their  culmination always changes the dynamic  of a show with the tension gone viewers  theoretically have less of a reason to  tune in and writers then have to come up  with new storylines Drescher initially  had no interest in having Fran pair up  with Max but she was forced into the  decision after the network threatened  cancellation as she put it in 2017 when  a show is built around a love that can't  happen you have to keep it that way she  may have just been right about that as  ratings plummeted after the wedding and  the show was off the air a season later  Veronica's closet was never a critical  darling but that didn't matter at least  for a while when a show on NBC is  produced by the same team that made  friends it automatically has some sway  instead of good reviews Veronica's  closet got something better a 9:30 p.m.  Thursday night timeslot on NBC  this was smack dab in the middle of the  must-see TV lineup right between  Seinfeld and ER during its first season  and between Frasier and ER during its  second ratings were great during this  time until they started to taper off  towards the end of season two bad  critical notice also caught up to it and  audiences were growing less and less  interested NBC responded to this ratings  dip by transferring Veronica's closet to  Monday evenings alongside suddenly Susan  some observers saw this as a way to dump  shows that were underperforming NBC had  openly promised critics and fans that  the show would improve and they even  brought on new writers from friends to  help it out ultimately none of this  tinkering mattered as the monday  timeslot killed it without a loyal  following Veronica's closet cratered in  the ratings on its way  getting cancelled suddenly Susan had a  lot going for it it had the support of a  major network a solid cast headlined by  Brooke Shields and one of the best time  slots on TV season one aired on Thursday  nights at 9:30 p.m.  right between Seinfeld and ER when it  was moved to Mondays the ratings took a  steep drop though they were still decent  but around the end of the third season  suddenly Susan was facing many  challenges cast member David Strickland  took his own life which was a major blow  to the rest of the cast and crew  furthermore during the off season almost  the entire creative team left as did  actor Judd Nelson he was replaced in the  final season by Eric Idle but it wasn't  enough between an unfamiliar creative  team in an unfavorable time slot  suddenly Susan dropped in the ratings  like a stone and it was canceled  midseason the remaining episodes were  burned off in the summer check out one  of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about your  favorite stuff coming soon subscribe to  our YouTube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one  [Music]

